      <li>Offer it to third parties as part of a product or service of your own;</li>
      <li>Operate it as shared infrastructure for an organisation or user base you serve; or</li>
      <li>Register accounts, environments, or tunnels on behalf of other people.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you want to provide something like RAS Connect to other people, the source is MIT-licensed
      and you are welcome to run your own relay.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="content">
    <p class="section-number">05</p>
    <h2>Your Content and Your Providers</h2>
    <p>
      Your prompts, source code, files, and the responses your coding agents produce stay between
      your clients, your own RAS Code server, and the agent and model providers you configure. RAS
      Connect is not in that path and takes no rights over that content. You keep everything you
      already own.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you enable notifications, your environment sends short activity summaries so a
      notification can be composed, as described in the
      <a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy Policy</a>. You grant a limited permission to store,
      transmit, and display those summaries for the purpose of delivering the notifications you asked
      for, and for nothing else.
    </p>
    <p>
      You are responsible for having the right to use the content you put through RAS Code, and for
      reviewing the terms and data practices of the agents and providers you connect to it before you
